Q: Graph structure - are pointers bad? A: <p>The existing answers are all <em>correct</em>, but the origin of the question is a fundamental misunderstanding of pointers, that the answers do not clearly straighten out, so please allow me to clarify:</p>
<p>Pointers are great. Pointers are a way to point at another piece of data.  You should definitely, <em>definitely</em> be using pointers on a regular basis.</p>
<p>However: A <em>lot</em> of schools teach pointers and manual memory management (<code>new</code> and <code>delete</code>)at the same time, and a lot of people assume that pointers and manual memory management go hand-in-hand.  But they shouldn't. And manual memory management is very easy to make mistakes with. If you go back and review the posts and comments about &quot;pointers are bad&quot;, and look closely, what you'll find is that what they are <em>actually</em> communicating is that <em>manual-memory management</em> is bad.  (Ergo, their followup is to use standard containers, <code>std::unique_ptr</code>, and <code>std::shared_ptr</code>)</p>
<p>When designing a data structure like this, the real question isn't &quot;should I use pointers&quot;, but is actually object <em>ownership</em>: &quot;which single object owns this data?&quot;  Should Nodes <em>own</em> the Edges? You don't want two owners, so maybe Nodes should only own outgoing Edges?  But with some Nodes refer to Edges owned by other Nodes, which makes it very easy for one Node to delete an Edge, but another Node still refers to it, so that's probably not a good idea.  Who owns the Nodes?  Presumably you have a Graph class that owns the Nodes, so it should probably also own the Edges.  These are the real questions.  And once you answer ownership, you end up with something like this:</p>
<pre><code>struct Edge {
    NodeRef from;
    NodeRef to;
};
struct Node {
    Data data;
    Container&lt;EdgeRef&gt; edges;
}
struct Node {
    Container&lt;Node&gt; nodes;
    Container&lt;Edge&gt; edges;
};
</code></pre>
<p>Then the next set of questions is about <em>lifetimes</em>. Presumably, NodeRef and EdgeRef are probably going to be (non-owning) <code>Node*</code> and <code>Edge*</code>, and generally, <code>std::vector</code> is usually the default Container for most things. But since there are pointers to the nodes and edges themselves, then if new nodes can be added and removed, then <code>std::vector</code> might move items around in memory, invalidating pointers.  So for this case, we probably don't want <code>std::vector</code>, and probably want a node-based container.  Possibly <code>std::set</code> or <code>std::map</code>, but interestingly, this is one of the rare cases where <code>std::list</code> can actually be a good fit as well.  And, since we have a node based container, it might actually be helpful for Nodes and Edges to refer to the nodes instead of the data themselves, so maybe the NodeRef and EdgeRef might be better off as a <code>std::list&lt;*&gt;::iterator</code>, which makes it easier to remove items.</p>
<p>So you can see, the reality is not that &quot;pointers are bad&quot;, so much as it is that you should really be thinking about ownership and lifetimes.  If you've sorted those out, then almost all dangerous uses of pointers simply no longer exist.  For the rare cases that remain, use <code>std::unique_ptr</code>. Or in <em>extremely</em> rare cases, <code>std::shared_ptr</code>, but that's almost always a sign that the developer didn't think through ownership fully.</p>

Q: Discord.js how to add role to a specific user ID? A: <p>The issue seems to be the lack of asyncronous handling. Besides that the <a href="https://discord.js.org/docs/packages/discord.js/14.27.0/ClientEvents:Interface#ready" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>ready</code> event is deprecated</a>, your code lacks of any <code>await</code>: this could lead to have variables treated as <code>undefined</code> since some function has yet to return that object.</p>
<p>As per today, the <code>get</code> method can't be found neither:</p>
<ul>
<li>in the <a href="https://discord.js.org/docs/packages/discord.js/14.27.0/GuildManager:Class" rel="nofollow noreferrer">docs of <code>GuildManager</code></a> (returned by <code>bot.guilds</code>),</li>
<li>in the <a href="https://discord.js.org/docs/packages/discord.js/14.27.0/RoleManager:Class" rel="nofollow noreferrer">docs of <code>RoleManager</code></a> (returned by <code>guild.roles</code>),</li>
<li>and neither in the <a href="https://discord.js.org/docs/packages/discord.js/14.27.0/GuildMemberManager:Class" rel="nofollow noreferrer">docs of <code>GuildMember</code></a> (returned by <code>guild.members</code>);</li>
</ul>
<p>It is, however, documented the <code>fetch</code> method instead, which returns a <a href="https://discord.js.org/docs/packages/discord.js/14.27.0/Guild:Class#fetch" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Promise&lt;Guild&gt;</code></a>, a <a href="https://discord.js.org/docs/packages/discord.js/14.27.0/RoleManager:Class#fetch" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Promise&lt;Role&gt;</code></a> and a <a href="https://discord.js.org/docs/packages/discord.js/14.27.0/Guild:Class#fetch" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Promise&lt;GuildMember&gt;</code></a> respectively.</p>
<p>So, your code should look like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>bot.on('clientReady', () =&gt; {
    // To clarify further, I'll comment each line with the representing classes
    // Client.GuildManager.fetch()
    const guild = await bot.guilds.fetch(GUILD_ID);
    // Guild.RoleManager.fetch()
    let role = await guild.roles.fetch(ROLE_ID);
    // Guild.GuildMemberManager.fetch()
    let user = await guild.members.fetch(USER_ID);
    // GuildMember.GuildMemberRoleManager.add()
    user.roles.add(role);
});
</code></pre>
<p><strong>N.B.</strong> in this simple example there are no null checks nor any validation, but keep in mind that, for example, <a href="https://discord.js.org/docs/packages/discord.js/14.27.0/RoleManager:Class#fetch" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>RoleManager.fetch</code> could also return <code>null</code></a>.</p>
